**Describe the bug**

After upgrading from v6 to v7, a query that populates a nested to-one relation (`account.student`) alongside a to-many relation (`items`) in the same `populate` array returns a `student` object where scalar fields (e.g. `fullName`) are empty/undefined, even though the relation itself is populated (the entity reference and its primary key are present).

In v6, the same query with the same populate array returned `student.fullName` correctly. The default loading strategy changed from `joined` (v6) to `balanced` (v7), and reverting the query's `strategy` option to `'joined'` explicitly restores the correct behavior. This suggests the `balanced` strategy is not correctly hydrating scalar fields on nested to-one relations when a to-many relation is present in the same populate hint.

**To Reproduce**

Entities (simplified from a production schema):

```ts
@Entity()
class User {
  @PrimaryKey()
  id!: number;

  @Property()
  email!: string;
}

@Entity()
class StudentBioPersonal {
  @PrimaryKey()
  id!: number;

  @Property()
  fullName!: string;

  @ManyToOne(() => User)
  user!: User;
}

@Entity()
class FinanceAccount {
  @PrimaryKey()
  id!: number;

  @ManyToOne(() => StudentBioPersonal)
  student!: StudentBioPersonal;
}

@Entity()
class FeeType {
  @PrimaryKey()
  id!: number;

  @Property()
  name!: string;
}

@Entity()
class InvoiceItem {
  @PrimaryKey()
  id!: number;

  @ManyToOne(() => Invoice)
  invoice!: Invoice;

  @ManyToOne(() => FeeType)
  feeType!: FeeType;
}

@Entity()
class Invoice {
  @PrimaryKey()
  id!: number;

  @ManyToOne(() => FinanceAccount)
  account!: FinanceAccount;

  @OneToMany(() => InvoiceItem, (item) => item.invoice)
  items = new Collection<InvoiceItem>(this);
}
```

Query (default `balanced` strategy, v7):

```ts
const [invoices] = await em.findAndCount(
  Invoice,
  {},
  {
    populate: ['account', 'account.student', 'account.student.user', 'items', 'items.feeType'],
    limit: 20,
    offset: 0,
  },
);

console.log(invoices[0].account.student.fullName); // expected: "John Doe", actual: undefined
console.log(invoices[0].account.student.id);        // present and correct
```

**Expected behavior**

`account.student.fullName` (and other scalar fields on the nested to-one relation) should be populated, matching v6 behavior.

**Workaround (confirmed)**

Explicitly forcing `strategy: 'joined'` on the to-one populate paths restores correct hydration of scalar fields. Additionally, when the query also needs pagination (`limit`/`offset`) alongside a to-many relation (`items`), that to-many relation must be populated in a **separate** call after the paginated root query, otherwise the JOIN-based row fan-out from the to-many relation breaks `limit`/`offset` on the root entity:

```ts
const [invoices, total] = await em.findAndCount(
  Invoice,
  {},
  {
    populate: ['account', 'account.student', 'account.student.user'],
    strategy: 'joined',
    limit: 20,
    offset: 0,
  },
);

// populate the to-many relation separately, after pagination is applied to the root entity
if (invoices.length > 0) {
  await em.populate(invoices, ['items', 'items.feeType']);
}

console.log(invoices[0].account.student.fullName); // correct with this workaround
```

Without `strategy: 'joined'`, `account.student.fullName` is undefined even though `account.student.id` is present. Without splitting `items` into a separate `em.populate()` call, combining `items` in the same `populate` array as `limit`/`offset` also risks incorrect pagination counts due to JOIN row fan-out on the to-many side.

**Environment**

- MikroORM version(s) affected: <!-- fill in exact version, e.g. 7.x.x -->
- MikroORM version before upgrade (working): <!-- e.g. 6.x.x -->
- Driver: `@mikro-orm/postgresql` (via `@mikro-orm/sql`)
- Node.js version: <!-- fill in -->
- `loadStrategy` explicitly configured? <!-- no / yes, value -->
- Full generated SQL for the failing query (enable `debug: true` or logger): <!-- paste here -->

**Additional context**

This may be related to the default loading strategy change documented in the [[v6→v7 upgrade guide](https://mikro-orm.io/docs/upgrading-v6-to-v7#default-loading-strategy-is-balanced)](https://mikro-orm.io/docs/upgrading-v6-to-v7#default-loading-strategy-is-balanced), where SQL drivers moved from `joined` (always JOIN) to `balanced` (JOIN for to-one, separate query for to-many). It's possible that when a to-many relation triggers a separate select-in query, the hydration/merge step for a nested to-one relation populated via JOIN in the same call does not correctly map scalar columns back onto the entity.

Hello, 

After upgrading from MikroORM 6.5.7 → 7.0.11, our MySQL connection count ramps up under load and never drops back down. Under v6 it would peak (e.g. ~120) and naturally drain to baseline; under v7 it stays at the peak indefinitely.

After investigation the root cause is the connection pool change from Knex/tarn.js to Kysely/mysql2's native pool, which has a different idle-cleanup behaviour. 

Not sure as this is a bug or just undocumented change.

In mysql2's BasePool constructor there is a condition that enables removing idle connections
See: https://github.com/sidorares/node-mysql2/blob/master/lib/base/pool.js#L50
```
  if (this.config.maxIdle < this.config.connectionLimit) {
    // create idle connection timeout automatically release job
    this._removeIdleTimeoutConnections();
  }
```

So by default new connections are created as needed and old connections are re-used and never removed as `maxIdle = connectionLimit`

To get back to the behaviour prior version 7 we had to add 

```json
  pool: {
    idleTimeoutMillis: 30000,
  },
  driverOptions: {
    maxIdle: 10,
  },
```

We had to use the driverOptions to set this value, we run crons and queues and each having it's own "pool" not releasing . For reference there is this issue on the mysql2 issue tracker: https://github.com/sidorares/node-mysql2/issues/2493

Do you have any recommendations on this part? Is this maxIdle not being set normal and would you suggest it we should keep the idle connections alive? 
Very simple load test shows a slight drop in performance as expected in having less idle connections.

Thanks,

